    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 232243\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n343 PM PDT Sun Aug 23 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Hotter than normal temperatures will continue through the week.\n\n* Chances for isolated showers and thunderstorms will linger through \n  Monday for portions of our forecast area. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through Saturday.\n\nPersistent ridging across the Desert Southwest will keep \ntemperatures above normal through the week. Monday will be the \n\"coolest\" day of the week, with afternoon high temperatures topping \nout around 2 top 5 degrees above normal. Temperatures across the\nregion will increase on Tuesday and Wednesday as we dry out. \nAcross the Mojave Desert, temperatures will be around 4 to 7 \ndegrees above normal through the second half of the week, with \ntemperatures around 2 to 4 degrees above normal across the \nsouthern Great Basin and in the Owens Valley. The long duration of\nthese anomalous temperatures will result in widespread Major-to- \nExtreme Heat Risk across the Mojave Desert during the latter half \nof the week with widespread Moderate Heat Risk across the southern\nGreat Basin and Owens Valley.\n\nCurrent radar and satellite imagery show isolated showers and \nthunderstorms moving southwest to northeast through Mohave County \nand across the southern Great Basin. Storms will continue to develop \non the higher terrain across northwestern Arizona and the southern \nGreat Basin this afternoon, with the potential for isolated \nconvection to pop up in the higher terrain of Clark County an the \nMojave Preserve. Gusty outflow winds, localized downpours, and \nlightning are the main threats with today's storms. Overnight, \nmoisture will increase across our southern and eastern zones. This\nadditional moisture will help to fuel another round of afternoon \nshowers and thunderstorms for areas south and east of the I-15 \ncorridor tomorrow. Beyond Monday, dry southwesterly flow aloft \nwill cause moisture to decrease across the region through the end \nof the week.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Gusty\nsouthwesterly winds continue through sunset, after which speeds \nwill fall to less than 10 knots out of the southwest. Isolated \nshowers and thunderstorms may develop along high terrain in the \nregion, with a low (<10%) chance of erratic outflow winds \nreaching the terminal. For tomorrow, winds will favor a \nsoutheasterly direction under 10 knots in the late morning, \nshifting to the southwest in the afternoon with gusts over 20 \nknots possible. Gusty outflow winds from area thunderstorms are \npossible again tomorrow afternoon, particularly from the \nsoutheast. Temperatures exceed 100 degrees until 04Z this evening,\nthen increase to above 100 degrees by 18Z tomorrow.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Gusty southerly winds\nwill weaken after sunset in the Las Vegas Valley, Colorado River \nValley, and Owens Valley. At the same time, gusty westerly winds \nwill develop at KDAG. Thunderstorm activity continues to bring the\nthreat of gusty outflow winds through the early evening, along \nwith lightning near the KBIH terminal. Another round of \nthunderstorms will begin tomorrow afternoon, primarily south and \neast of Interstate 15. Confidence is low in storms reaching any \nparticular terminal - gusty and erratic outflow winds are a \ngreater concern.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Stessman\nAVIATION...Meltzer\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
